'''''Agramunt v. Balboa Pension Fund''''' (2006–2007) was a landmark {{w|court case}} in [[Balboa]] in which [[Marc Agramunt i Riera]], a resident of [[Sant Cristòfor]], challenged the denial of survivor {{w|pension}} benefits for his deceased civil partner. The [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] ruled in Agramunt's favour, establishing that {{w|civil union|civil partnerships}} must carry equivalent legal weight to {{w|marriage}} in matters of state-administered benefits. The ruling was widely credited as the catalyst for the Marriage Equality Act of 2011.

== Background ==

[[Marc Agramunt i Riera]] and his partner had been in a registered {{w|civil union|civil partnership}} since 2005, the year after the Civil Partnership Act was passed. The Act granted registered same-sex couples legal recognition, {{w|inheritance}} rights, next-of-kin status, and limited parental rights. When Agramunt's partner died in 2006, Agramunt applied for survivor pension benefits through the [[Balboa Pension Fund]], the state-administered pension scheme.

The Balboa Pension Fund denied the application on the grounds that survivor benefits were available only to married couples. Although the Civil Partnership Act had granted civil partners next-of-kin status and inheritance rights, it had not explicitly addressed pension benefits, and the Balboa Pension Fund's regulations recognised survivor benefits only for spouses in a legal marriage.

== Case ==

Agramunt brought a case before the [[National Anti-Discrimination Tribunal]] in 2006, arguing that the exclusion of civil partners from survivor benefits constituted {{w|discrimination}} on the basis of relationship status and, indirectly, on the basis of sexual orientation. Agramunt's legal team argued that the Civil Partnership Act's grant of next-of-kin status implied parity with marriage in all matters of state-administered benefits, and that the Balboa Pension Fund's interpretation created a two-tier system in which civil partners were treated as second-class partners.

The Balboa Pension Fund defended its policy on the grounds that the Civil Partnership Act did not explicitly address pension benefits and that the Institute's regulations, which predated the Act, defined survivor benefits by reference to marriage. The Institute argued that the remedy lay with the legislature, not the Tribunal.

== Ruling ==

The Tribunal ruled in Agramunt's favour in 2007, finding that the pension scheme's exclusion of civil partners was discriminatory. The Tribunal ordered the Balboa Pension Fund to extend survivor benefits to registered civil partners and to process Agramunt's application retroactively.

The ruling established the principle that civil partnerships must carry equivalent legal weight to marriage in matters of state-administered benefits. The Tribunal held that the Civil Partnership Act's grant of legal recognition to same-sex couples created a reasonable expectation of parity with marriage in all matters of state-administered benefits, and that the absence of explicit pension provisions in the Act did not permit the Balboa Pension Fund to exclude civil partners.

== Significance ==

The Agramunt ruling was significant because it exposed the inherent inequality of the civil partnership framework. By establishing that civil partners were entitled to equivalent benefits to married couples, the ruling created a legal inconsistency: if civil partnerships carried the same legal weight as marriage in matters of state benefits, the separate framework was functionally redundant. Proponents of {{w|same-sex marriage|marriage equality}} cited the Agramunt ruling as evidence that the civil partnership system was inherently unequal and that the only remedy was to extend marriage rights to same-sex couples.

The ruling is widely credited as the catalyst for the Marriage Equality Act, which was passed in 2011 and extended full marriage rights to same-sex couples. The Agramunt case was also cited in the campaign for the [[Constitutional Amendment on Sexual Orientation Protections]] (2008), which added sexual orientation to the protected categories under the [[Twelve Guarantees]].

